Timely access to GP, community health and mental health services remains uneven, especially for people in deprived neighbourhoods and those with limited English. To improve access quickly, I would prioritise targeted resources that respond to actual local needs, including:
- interpreters and bilingual staff embedded in primary care and mental health teams;
- ongoing cultural competence training for receptionists, nurses and clinicians;
- proactive outreach in communities to explain what services exist and how to use them;
- flexible hours, walk-in slots and extended GP surgeries in convenient, local settings;
- community health workers who can guide patients through referrals and follow‑ups;
- multilingual information, easy-read materials and user-friendly online access;
- mobile clinics and home-visit pilots in hard-to-reach areas;
- streamlined booking with interpreters available by phone or video.

In my area, a neighbour waiting weeks for a GP appointment and a relative struggling to access culturally appropriate counselling showed how language and culture shape access. If resources are clearly targeted to reduce disparities and monitored with patient feedback, we should see faster, fairer care. But funding must come with transparency and guardrails to prevent gaps from reappearing in future.

absolutely i recognise the aim of getting GP, community health and mental health services closer together so people can get help more quickly. But I’m not convinced that investing in large, co‑located community hubs is the best way to achieve timely access in my area. There are real risks and practical problems that could end up slowing things down or shifting access away from where people already go for care.

First, it’s a big upfront and ongoing cost. Building or refurbishing hubs, running them, and keeping IT systems, admin and governance working across NHS teams, social care and VCSE partners would drain funding from frontline services just when we need more capacity. If the money is tied up in capital and complex setup, there’s less left to recruit and retain GPs, nurses, and mental health professionals who are actually seeing patients now. I’ve seen small pockets of investment that promised faster access but didn’t translate into more same‑day GP appointments because staffing simply can’t keep up with demand.

Second, integration across so many organisations is hard in practice. You’ve got NHS services, local authorities, housing, social care, and countless voluntary groups, each with different computer systems, data protection rules and performance metrics. Interoperability work sounds sensible on paper, but in reality it creates admin bottlenecks, delays in sharing information and a lot of duplicated processes. If a patient moves between services, there’s a real risk of communication gaps and care being less continuous rather than more coherent.

Third, access and equity concerns matter. A hub in a town centre may look central, but for many people—those who rely on public transport, have caring responsibilities, or work long hours—the hub could be harder to reach than a local GP practice. Parking, travel time, and inconvenient appointment windows can negate any supposed speed gains. And for people used to building a relationship with their own GP, having to switch to a different team in a central hub could undermine continuity of care, which is often crucial for mental health and long‑term conditions.

Fourth, there’s a real risk of drawing staff away from existing practices. If multi‑disciplinary teams are pooled into hubs, smaller GP surgeries could be left with fewer clinicians and longer waits. In my experience, the best care comes from established local teams who know you and your history. A hub model can unintentionally fragment that continuity, unless there are very strong safeguards, which themselves add to complexity and cost.

Fifth, evidence and governance aren’t straightforward. There isn’t a clear, universal recipe proving that hubs reliably cut waiting times across all areas. What works in one city centre might not work in a suburban neighbourhood. We’d need robust pilots with independent evaluation, sunset clauses, and hard data on wait times, patient satisfaction, and outcomes before committing to scale. Without that, funds could be spent on buildings that don’t deliver real improvements.

What would work better in my view is strengthening existing primary and community services where people already have relationships. This could include:
- boosting GP capacity with more doctors, physician associates and extended hours in every practice; 
- improved same‑day access through enhanced triage and digital booking, so people can see someone quickly when they need to; 
- more targeted mental health crisis teams and rapid response services that operate across the local area, not just within a hub; 
- expanding the role of community pharmacists and linked social prescribing with clear funding streams to VCSE groups, so people can get practical and social support without overwhelming clinical services; 
- simpler, better‑governed data sharing that protects privacy but truly speeds up care, with patient consent as a default, not an afterthought.

I’m not against collaboration between NHS services, social care and VCSEs. I am against assuming that co‑located hubs are a silver bullet that will automatically improve timeliness or access. If hubs are pursued, they must be narrowly targeted, locally driven, and rigorously evaluated from day one, with explicit safeguards for continuity of care, equity of access, and long‑term sustainability. For me, the priority should be to fix what we already have first—robust core primary care, real same‑day access, and well‑coordinated mental health support—before pouring money into a new consolidation model that could complicate access rather than simplify it.

Thank you for the chance to feed in on this. As a youth-focused organisation operating across England, we welcome the aim to improve timely access to GP, community health and mental health services. However, we’re concerned that the changes being talked about will not deliver meaningful improvements in the short term unless they are backed by solid funding, practical delivery plans and real workforce retention – not just more training places on paper. The biggest issue we see from young people is not only the number of staff in the system, but how available and approachable those services actually are when someone needs them.

First, about training places, return‑to‑practice support, apprenticeships and CPD. Yes, expanding the pipeline of GPs, nurses and mental health professionals is essential for the long term. But simply creating training slots without guaranteeing job posts, fair pay, safe working conditions and viable career progression will not translate into quicker access for patients. In our networks we hear stories of graduates who train for years only to end up working in temporary roles or leaving the NHS because of workload, stress and lack of certainty. If you expand training, you must accompany it with:
- guaranteed posts and clear career pathways after qualification;
- sustained funding to support safe staffing levels and manageable workloads; and
- robust return‑to‑practice packages with mentorship and smoothing of red tape so staff can re‑enter the system quickly and safely. Apprenticeships should be meaningful routes into patient‑facing roles, not tokens; they must include high‑quality supervision, consistent rotation through core services, and recognition of prior experience.

Second, expansion of community mental health teams, early intervention, crisis response and talking therapies. We agree these are crucial to reducing waits and bringing parity with physical health. But there are real caveats. Expansion must be regionally equitable and adequately funded, not just “more staff” without the spaces, equipment and time to see people properly. Early intervention is effective, but only if it is youth‑specific and accessible through schools, universities, and community hubs where young people actually seek help. Crisis response teams need to be available outside traditional office hours and across rural and urban areas alike. Talking therapies must be timely, culturally competent, and tailored for different age groups, including young adults who often fall between child and adult services. If expansions rely on short-term grants or over‑stretched teams, wait times will simply shift rather than shrink.

We also think the plan needs to be more explicit about integration. GP services, community health, mental health and social care do not operate in silos. Young people bounce between services for years, and missed handovers are common. Any change should include:
- integrated digital records and secure data sharing between primary care, mental health teams and schools/colleges; 
- joined‑up commissioning so investments in mental health do not come at the expense of other local services; 
- local workforce planning that recognises geography, with targeted recruitment drives in areas with the worst gaps; and
- youth‑participation in designing these services so that what is promised matches what is actually needed.

In practice, what would help most:
- ring‑fenced funding for training pipelines paired with guaranteed career posts and protection against under‑funded expansion; 
- clear, two‑way pathways for return‑to‑practice staff, including supervised practice time and mentoring; 
- regional development of multi‑disciplinary teams with social care links, school‑based mental health links, and crisis response that can operate evenings and weekends; 
- investment in digital triage, same‑day GP access where possible, and in-person slots for complex cases; 
- transparent wait‑time dashboards and published targets by area so communities know when improvements are happening.

From our perspective, the question isn’t whether to expand training or to grow mental health teams; it’s how to do it so the improvements are real, timely and sustainable for young people across every region. Without binding funding commitments, practical retention measures, and genuine service integration, the risk is that waits persist and trust in the NHS diminishes further. We urge policymakers to couple workforce expansion with concrete delivery plans that prioritise accessibility, equity and quality of care for all young people.
